{"lecture_id":"2026-07-13-M2-4","lecture_summary":"The lecture applies force and torque equations to three representative situations: a plank at the tipping threshold, a ladder limited by static friction, and an Atwood machine with a massive pulley. Its recurring strategy is to draw appropriate free-body diagrams, choose a pivot that eliminates unknown torques, combine translational and rotational equations, apply constraints such as a = αR, solve symbolically, and check the result physically.","videos":[{"rank":1,"title":"Static Equilibrium - Tension, Torque, Lever, Beam, & Ladder Problem - Physics","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qGvFAl5CK_c","reason":"The strongest compact match for the first two examples: it covers force and torque equilibrium, useful pivot choices, beams and levers, and a ladder involving friction."},{"rank":2,"title":"Rotational Dynamics - Basic Introduction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dbvr-L5rxdg","reason":"Directly matches the massive-pulley portion through rotating pulleys and Atwood machines, connecting Newton’s laws for the masses with rotational dynamics for the pulley."}],"coverage_gaps":["No playlist entry explicitly describes the lecture’s two-support tipping condition: the departing support force becomes zero and torque is balanced about the remaining support. The beam-and-lever material in the first selection is closely related, but this exact threshold method is not advertised."]}
